***********************************MANUAL DEPLOY***********************************

Complete Deployment Steps
1. Start Minikube and Configure Docker Environment
    # Start minikube
    minikube start

    # Point your Docker CLI to minikube's Docker daemon
        eval $(minikube docker-env)

2. Build Your Application Image
    From your project root directory (where the Dockerfile is located):
    bashdocker build -t homefinance:latest .
    This will:

    Use Maven to build your Spring Boot application
    Create a lightweight runtime image with just the JAR file
    Enable remote debugging on port 5005

3. Deploy to Kubernetes
    Deploy in this specific order:
    bash# Deploy MySQL first
    kubectl apply -f mysql-pvc.yaml
    kubectl apply -f mysql-deployment.yaml
    kubectl apply -f mysql-service.yaml

    # Wait for MySQL to be ready
    kubectl wait --for=condition=ready pod -l app=mysql --timeout=300s

    # Deploy your application
    kubectl apply -f homefinance-deployment.yaml
    kubectl apply -f homefinance-service.yaml
4. Verify Deployment
    Check that everything is running:
    bash# Check pods status
    kubectl get pods

    # Check services
    kubectl get services

    # Check logs if needed
    kubectl logs -l app=homefinance
    kubectl logs -l app=mysql
5. Access Your Application
    Get the URLs to access your application:
    bash# Get service URLs
    minikube service homefinance --url
    This will show you something like:

    http://192.168.49.2:30XXX (for port 8585 - your main application)
    http://192.168.49.2:30YYY (for port 5005 - debug port)

Notes About Your Setup
    Debugging: Your application is configured with remote debugging enabled on port 5005. You can connect your IDE to this port for debugging.
    Database Connection: Your app will connect to MySQL at jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/homefinancedb - this should work correctly with your service configuration.
    Environment: The SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=dev environment variable is set, so make sure your application has the appropriate dev profile configuration.
    Troubleshooting
    If you encounter issues:
    bash# Check pod logs
    kubectl logs deployment/homefinance
    kubectl logs deployment/mysql

# Check pod descriptions for more details
    kubectl describe pod -l app=homefinance
    kubectl describe pod -l app=mysql

# Check if the image was built correctly
    docker images | grep homefinance
    Clean Up (when needed)
    To remove everything:
    bashkubectl delete -f homefinance-service.yaml
    kubectl delete -f homefinance-deployment.yaml
    kubectl delete -f mysql-service.yaml
    kubectl delete -f mysql-deployment.yaml
    kubectl delete -f mysql-pvc.yaml
    Your Dockerfile is well-structured with a multi-stage build that keeps the final image size small while including debugging capabilities. The setup should work smoothly with your Kubernetes configuration!